# Figure 1 setup: a cryptic site every detector finds and only one ranks.
#
# Mycobacterium tuberculosis adenosine kinase. 2PKF is the apo form, 2PKK the same
# protein and chain with 2-fluoroadenosine bound. Substrate binding moves the site
# residues 2.8 A on average (3.65 A global CA RMSD after superposition), which is
# the conformational change that makes the site cryptic.
#
# All three detectors propose a qualifying candidate here; only the ranking
# differs. fpocket puts it at rank 13 of 17, P2Rank at rank 1, Lacuna at rank 2.
# Under top-5 recovery fpocket is scored as having missed it entirely.
#
# Chosen over the hexokinase pair (4JAX/3O8M) after measuring both: hexokinase
# moves only 1.86 A globally, which does not read at surface rendering scale, and
# its apo cleft is wider than its holo one, which inverts the picture a reader
# expects. This pair moves twice as far in the conventional direction.
#
# Saving is a separate step, below, because both panels must share one camera. If
# the view changes between them the reader sees a change of framing rather than a
# change of conformation, which is the one thing the figure exists to show.
#
#   open C:/Users/clayt/Documents/GitHub/lacuna/paper/figures/fig1_adenosine_kinase.cxc

close all
cd C:/Users/clayt/Documents/GitHub/lacuna/paper/figures

set bgColor white
lighting soft
graphics silhouettes true width 1.2

open 2pkf
delete #1 & ~/A
open 2pkk
delete #2 & ~/A

# One frame of reference for both states.
matchmaker #2 to #1

hide cartoons
hide atoms
show #1,2 surfaces
transparency #1,2 0 surfaces
color #1,2 #D8D8D2

# The eighteen residues CryptoBench annotates. Numbering is identical in both
# entries, so one list serves both.
color #1/A:8,10,12,47,48,49,52,102,116,121,146,147,172,173,253,254,257,293 #C8553D
color #2/A:8,10,12,47,48,49,52,102,116,121,146,147,172,173,253,254,257,293 #C8553D

# 2-fluoroadenosine, sized to read at figure scale.
show #2/A:501 atoms
style #2/A:501 sphere
size #2/A:501 atomRadius 1.5
color #2/A:501 #2E5E8E

# Frame on the site with the holo state showing, so the bound state is what you
# orient against.
hide #1 surfaces
view #2/A:8,10,12,47,48,49,52,102,116,121,146,147,172,173,253,254,257,293
zoom 0.6

# A selection outline renders into saved images, so clear it.
~select

# ── Rotate by hand until you are looking into the site. ───────────────────────
# Then paste the block below and do not touch the mouse between the two saves.
# Only surface visibility and the ligand toggle; the camera never moves.
#
# transparency #2 40 surfaces
# save panel_b.png width 2000 supersample 3 transparentBackground true
# transparency #2 0 surfaces
# hide #2/A:501 atoms
# hide #2 surfaces
# show #1 surfaces
# save panel_a.png width 2000 supersample 3 transparentBackground true
#
# The ligand is drawn as atoms, not as part of the surface, so hiding the holo
# surface alone leaves it floating in the apo panel. Hiding it explicitly is what
# keeps the two panels honest.
